1:44.741Z","session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"cb2b273d-43ee-4789-9789-2f7b74391526","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"54454a4d-12b9-4ee3-b2a6-9023849a1ade","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C3oUnfBPLRwYZmPdUpqYVX","type":"tool_result","content":"72:Exception, directed by the repository owner on 2026-10-08: the local usage host has a **Use local sign-ins** setting, on by default at the owner's request and visible in Settings. Only while it is on may it read the Claude Code and Factory Droid sign-ins already stored on that computer (keychain or credential files), read-only, and call the usage endpoints those CLIs use. It must never refresh, write, log, or return those tokens, and the UI must say the endpoints are undocumented. Keep every other source on documented paths.\n# Subset\n\nSubset is a collection of small, independently useful apps. Each app solves one focused problem and can, where its platform allows, also serve as a capability inside 21n products or as an interface returned by an AI agent.\n\nThe first pilots are a **usage dashboard** and a **PDF annotation and review tool**. The local usage preview supports isolated Codex/ChatGPT connections, separately collected Claude Code and Antigravity CLI quota snapshots, and authorized Cursor team-member spending reports. Personal Cursor subscription quota is not supported; neither pilot is released. The directory labels availability accordingly.\n\n## Product model\n\nA Subset capability has a clear user outcome, its own data and operations, and one or more delivery surfaces:\n\n| Surface | Example |\n| --- | --- |\n| Standalone web app | A usage dashboard opened from the Subset directory |\n| Embedded web view | The same usage view inside Nucleum or Outright |\n| Agent interface | A trusted, registered view shown beside a structured tool result |\n14:| Agent delivery | Structured result, optional registered view; separate local CLI/skill | Planned. Each host and the published CLI/skill require their own verification. |\n16:The usage dashboard boundary is normalized per-source status and explicit read operations. Its sources are ChatGPT-signed-in Codex homes through the documented local app-server protocol, independently collected Claude Code/Antigravity CLI quota snapshots, and authorized Cursor team-member spending through its official Admin API. Quota snapshots and spending caps remain distinct; personal Cursor subscriptions and API billing are not implemented quota sources. The standalone host owns profile persistence and collector bindings, while the package owns normalization and source reads. Proposed consumers are a standalone web host, an Outright adapter, a local CLI and skill for shell-capable agents, and a registered view in an agent host. The PDF review boundary is document identity, page/annotation operations, and explicit export; web, native, product, and agent views may differ. No capability may import an app or a consuming product.\n22:3. For usage, Codex uses one `CODEX_HOME` per sign-in with manual live reads. Claude Code and Antigravity use allowlisted status-line snapshots with explicit per-account collector bindings, and a Claude account can be bound to its own `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`; refresh does not advance snapshot observation times or query those providers. Cursor team profiles select an exact member ID and reference a server environment credential, never a browser token. The host provides account operations and keeps credentials out of rendered status. Live multi-account sign-ins, installed CLI compatibility, Cursor authorization, and external host behavior still need verification before any availability claim. See [the usage README](../packages/usage/README.md) and [SET-1 and its linked planning documents](https://linear.app/21n/issue/SET-1/pilot-a-reusable-usage-status-dashboard).\n24:5. Keep catalog `availableSurfaces` empty until the actual artifact and route pass release checks. A published CLI/skill and a hosted agent view are separate claims. A localhost browser page is not an embedded agent view.\n1:# Usage capability (SET-1)\n3:This private package owns the version 1 per-account usage contract, read-only provider adapters, the registered view ID `subset.usage.dashboard@1`, a structured agent result with a text fallback, and the shared Svelte dashboard view. The standalone host is `apps/usage`. No public surface is released. External view inputs are validated with `isUsageStatus()` before the standalone host renders them.\n5:## Supported local sources\n25:## Local sign-ins (opt-in)\n35:## Codex banked resets\n39:## Codex account boundary\n49:## Standalone local preview\n66:### Paid credits and extra usage\n70:### Dashboard view\n72:The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.\n76:## Claude and Antigravity collectors\n88:    \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/subset/apps/usage/server.mjs collect --account claude_example\"\n107:## Cursor team accounts\n113:## Validation and release gaps\n117:## Superfunctions reuse review","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"42c40a72-a1e0-438d-8638-f8ccaccadfcd","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13:21:44.847Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"72:Exception, directed by the repository owner on 2026-10-08: the local usage host has a **Use local sign-ins** setting, on by default at the owner's request and visible in Settings. Only while it is on may it read the Claude Code and Factory Droid sign-ins already stored on that computer (keychain or credential files), read-only, and call the usage endpoints those CLIs use. It must never refresh, write, log, or return those tokens, and the UI must say the endpoints are undocumented. Keep every other source on documented paths.\n# Subset\n\nSubset is a collection of small, independently useful apps. Each app solves one focused problem and can, where its platform allows, also serve as a capability inside 21n products or as an interface returned by an AI agent.\n\nThe first pilots are a **usage dashboard** and a **PDF annotation and review tool**. The local usage preview supports isolated Codex/ChatGPT connections, separately collected Claude Code and Antigravity CLI quota snapshots, and authorized Cursor team-member spending reports. Personal Cursor subscription quota is not supported; neither pilot is released. The directory labels availability accordingly.\n\n## Product model\n\nA Subset capability has a clear user outcome, its own data and operations, and one or more delivery surfaces:\n\n| Surface | Example |\n| --- | --- |\n| Standalone web app | A usage dashboard opened from the Subset directory |\n| Embedded web view | The same usage view inside Nucleum or Outright |\n| Agent interface | A trusted, registered view shown beside a structured tool result |\n14:| Agent delivery | Structured result, optional registered view; separate local CLI/skill | Planned. Each host and the published CLI/skill require their own verification. |\n16:The usage dashboard boundary is normalized per-source status and explicit read operations. Its sources are ChatGPT-signed-in Codex homes through the documented local app-server protocol, independently collected Claude Code/Antigravity CLI quota snapshots, and authorized Cursor team-member spending through its official Admin API. Quota snapshots and spending caps remain distinct; personal Cursor subscriptions and API billing are not implemented quota sources. The standalone host owns profile persistence and collector bindings, while the package owns normalization and source reads. Proposed consumers are a standalone web host, an Outright adapter, a local CLI and skill for shell-capable agents, and a registered view in an agent host. The PDF review boundary is document identity, page/annotation operations, and explicit export; web, native, product, and agent views may differ. No capability may import an app or a consuming product.\n22:3. For usage, Codex uses one `CODEX_HOME` per sign-in with manual live reads. Claude Code and Antigravity use allowlisted status-line snapshots with explicit per-account collector bindings, and a Claude account can be bound to its own `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`; refresh does not advance snapshot observation times or query those providers. Cursor team profiles select an exact member ID and reference a server environment credential, never a browser token. The host provides account operations and keeps credentials out of rendered status. Live multi-account sign-ins, installed CLI compatibility, Cursor authorization, and external host behavior still need verification before any availability claim. See [the usage README](../packages/usage/README.md) and [SET-1 and its linked planning documents](https://linear.app/21n/issue/SET-1/pilot-a-reusable-usage-status-dashboard).\n24:5. Keep catalog `availableSurfaces` empty until the actual artifact and route pass release checks. A published CLI/skill and a hosted agent view are separate claims. A localhost browser page is not an embedded agent view.\n1:# Usage capability (SET-1)\n3:This private package owns the version 1 per-account usage contract, read-only provider adapters, the registered view ID `subset.usage.dashboard@1`, a structured agent result with a text fallback, and the shared Svelte dashboard view. The standalone host is `apps/usage`. No public surface is released. External view inputs are validated with `isUsageStatus()` before the standalone host renders them.\n5:## Supported local sources\n25:## Local sign-ins (opt-in)\n35:## Codex banked resets\n39:## Codex account boundary\n49:## Standalone local preview\n66:### Paid credits and extra usage\n70:### Dashboard view\n72:The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.\n76:## Claude and Antigravity collectors\n88:    \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/subset/apps/usage/server.mjs collect --account claude_example\"\n107:## Cursor team accounts\n113:## Validation and release gaps\n117:## Superfunctions reuse review","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cb2b273d-43ee-4789-9789-2f7b74391526","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"42c40a72-a1e0-438d-8638-f8ccaccadfcd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01C3oUnfBPLRwYZmPdUpqYVX","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"if [ -z \"${HOME-}\" ]; then case \"${OSTYPE-}\" in msys*|cygwin*|win32*) printf '{}\\n'; if [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_PORT-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_PANE_KEY-}\" ]; then exit 0; fi; { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || : ;; *) { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; printf '{}\\n' ;; esac; else case \"${OSTYPE-}\" in msys*|cygwin*|win32*) if [ -f \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.cmd\" ]; then case \"${HOME-}\" in *\\&*|*\\^*|*\\(*|*\\)*|*\\;*|*,*|*=*|*%*|*\\!*) if [ -f \"${SYSTEMROOT-}/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe\" ]; then \"${SYSTEMROOT-}/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe\" -NoProfile -EncodedCommand ; else printf '{}\\n'; if [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_PORT-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_PANE_KEY-}\" ]; then exit 0; fi; { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; fi ;; *) \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.cmd\" ;; esac; else printf '{}\\n'; if [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_PORT-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_PANE_KEY-}\" ]; then exit 0; fi; { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; fi ;; *) if [ -f \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\" ] && [ -r \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\" ] && [ -x \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\" ]; then ORCA_HOOK_AGENT_PID=\"${PPID:-}\"; export ORCA_HOOK_AGENT_PID; /bin/sh \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\"; else { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; printf '{}\\n'; fi ;; esac; fi","durationMs":36},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f620b268-61e5-4776-b467-c5daa72b3014","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13