ents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"eafa5d8e-0278-4909-be9a-5cf136d0d8e7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01QMHbbno9swu2YmZYK9aTwf","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"if [ -z \"${HOME-}\" ]; then case \"${OSTYPE-}\" in msys*|cygwin*|win32*) printf '{}\\n'; if [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_PORT-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_PANE_KEY-}\" ]; then exit 0; fi; { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || : ;; *) { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; printf '{}\\n' ;; esac; else case \"${OSTYPE-}\" in msys*|cygwin*|win32*) if [ -f \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.cmd\" ]; then case \"${HOME-}\" in *\\&*|*\\^*|*\\(*|*\\)*|*\\;*|*,*|*=*|*%*|*\\!*) if [ -f \"${SYSTEMROOT-}/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe\" ]; then \"${SYSTEMROOT-}/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe\" -NoProfile -EncodedCommand ; else printf '{}\\n'; if [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_PORT-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_PANE_KEY-}\" ]; then exit 0; fi; { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; fi ;; *) \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.cmd\" ;; esac; else printf '{}\\n'; if [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_PORT-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN-}\" ] || [ -z \"${ORCA_PANE_KEY-}\" ]; then exit 0; fi; { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; fi ;; *) if [ -f \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\" ] && [ -r \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\" ] && [ -x \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\" ]; then ORCA_HOOK_AGENT_PID=\"${PPID:-}\"; export ORCA_HOOK_AGENT_PID; /bin/sh \"${HOME-}/.orca/agent-hooks/claude-hook.sh\"; else { command -p cat 2>/dev/null || cat; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || :; printf '{}\\n'; fi ;; esac; fi","durationMs":33},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3a7f75be-ec29-46bb-a4a8-8e8e9b0a102c","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13:21:46.690Z","session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"eafa5d8e-0278-4909-be9a-5cf136d0d8e7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"54454a4d-12b9-4ee3-b2a6-9023849a1ade","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QMHbbno9swu2YmZYK9aTwf","type":"tool_result","content":"## Local sign-ins (opt-in)\n\n**Settings → Use local sign-ins** is on by default (the repository owner's choice) and stored server-side in `usage-preferences.json`; turning it off stops all reads of stored sign-ins. When on:\n\n- **Claude Code:** for each account bound to a config directory, the host first runs `claude auth status` (Claude Code refreshes its own token there), then reads the stored OAuth access token from the macOS keychain item `Claude Code-credentials` (default directory) or `Claude Code-credentials-<first 8 hex of SHA-256 of the directory>`, falling back to `<dir>/.credentials.json`. It calls `https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage` read-only and shows `five_hour`, `seven_day`, and `limits[]` entries of kind `weekly_scoped` (per-model weekly limits such as Fable). Subset never calls the token endpoint: Anthropic rotates refresh tokens, so refreshing would sign Claude Code out. An expired token asks the user to open Claude Code. If no stored sign-in is found, the status-line snapshot is used.\n- **Factory Droid:** reads `<factory home>/.factory/auth.v2.loginkeychain`, `auth.v2.keyring`, or `auth.v2.file`, decrypts it (AES-256-GCM) with the key from the keychain item `Factory CLI` or `auth.v2.key`, and calls `https://api.factory.ai/api/billing/limits` (or the EU host) with the account's organization. When off, a Droid account needs a Factory API key variable; Subset then calls `/api/cli/whoami` for the organization and the same limits endpoint.\n- **Codex** is unchanged: Codex app-server already reads its own sign-in through a documented interface.\n\nNeither endpoint is documented by its provider, so either can change or stop working. Tokens stay in memory for one request and are never stored, logged, or sent to the view. macOS may show a keychain prompt the first time; choose **Always Allow** so auto-refresh does not wait on it. The approach follows Synara (MIT; `apps/server/src/providerUsage/providers`), Orca, and Zeron, which read the same stores. Subset's implementation is independent and read-only. Keychain lookup is macOS-only; elsewhere only credential files are read.\n\n## Standalone local preview\n\nIn the local dashboard, choose **Use current Codex account** to add the existing ChatGPT sign-in, then enter a label and choose **Connect another ChatGPT account**. The host starts the [documented Codex app-server browser login](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app-server) in a new, isolated Codex home. Follow **Continue with ChatGPT** and choose the intended account in the browser. The dashboard polls the login result and adds the account after Codex confirms a ChatGPT sign-in. A browser reload recovers the active sign-in ID, validated link, and state from the same local server, restoring progress and cancellation controls without persisting credentials or login state in browser storage. This recovery does not survive a server restart. Subset never sees or stores OAuth tokens; Codex manages them in its own home. **Remove** unlinks an existing Codex home from the dashboard; for a Subset-managed home it also deletes that local sign-in after explicit confirmation. Cancelling an unfinished login deletes only its newly created home.\n\nThe host stores profile labels and Codex home paths in `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` with owner-only file permissions. Managed Codex homes live under `~/.local/share/subset/codex-accounts/`, each with owner-only directory permissions. An owner-controlled profile file may also be supplied directly, for example:\n\n```json\n[\n  { \"id\": \"personal\", \"label\": \"Personal\", \"codexHome\": \"/absolute/path/to/personal-codex-home\" },\n  { \"id\": \"work\", \"label\": \"Work\", \"codexHome\": \"/absolute/path/to/work-codex-home\" }\n]\n```\n\nEach manually added home must already be signed in with its intended ChatGPT account using Codex. `CODEX_HOME=/absolute/path/to/personal-codex-home codex login` is the Codex-managed login path. `--profile` selects a config overlay within one home and is not account isolation. Do not copy auth files between homes. Account email is not stored in profile metadata or sent to the dashboard status view, so labels should distinguish accounts. A second real ChatGPT sign-in has not yet been available for live two-account verification.\n\nAfter `npm run build`, use `npm run status --workspace=@subset/usage-web` for structured JSON or `npm run serve --workspace=@subset/usage-web` for the local view at `http://127.0.0.1:4174`. `SUBSET_USAGE_PROFILES_FILE` selects a different JSON file; the existing `SUBSET_CODEX_PROFILES_FILE` remains a fallback for persisted Codex setups. The default stays `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` and accepts all provider types. `SUBSET_USAGE_DATA_DIR` optionally selects a managed data directory; `SUBSET_USAGE_PORT` changes the loopback port. Stop the server with Ctrl-C or SIGTERM. The server binds only to `127.0.0.1`, checks Host and Origin headers, and requires a matching browser Origin for account mutations. This is a local preview, not a published CLI/skill, hosted agent view, or public web app. OpenAI documents Codex app-server authentication for local or open-source applications, with a different authorization path required for commercial or hosted services.\n\n## Claude and Antigravity collectors\n\nIn **Manage accounts**, choose **Claude Code quota snapshot** or **Antigravity CLI quota snapshot**, give the account a distinct label, and choose **Add snapshot account**. Expand that account's collector setup and use its generated command, which includes its unique local ID and any custom profile file selection. The command reads JSON on stdin, writes an owner-only quota snapshot, and deliberately produces no stdout.\n\n**Automatic installation.** For a Claude Code account bound to a config directory, or an Antigravity account, **Install collector** (on the card, in account settings, or as a checkbox when adding the account) writes the collector into that CLI's `settings.json`. It is an explicit operation: Subset first copies the file to `settings.json.subset-backup`, keeps every other key, and saves the replaced `statusLine` in an owner-only Subset file. The collector then runs that previous status line with the same input, so an existing display (for example Orca's) keeps rendering. **Uninstall** or removing the account restores the previous `statusLine` unless another tool has since replaced it. Status-line collectors only run in CLI sessions started after installation; a session that was already open keeps its old settings. Account settings report whether the collector is installed, missing, or replaced by another tool. Each collector run records only its time and outcome (`saved`, `no-quota`, or `wrong-directory`) in an owner-only `collector-runs` directory, so the dashboard can say whether the collector has not run yet (for example, a CLI session opened before installation), ran without quota, or ran from the wrong config directory. Settings that are not valid JSON are never rewritten. An account without a config directory cannot be installed automatically.\n\nFor manual setup, configure that command as `statusLine.command` in the intended Claude Code account environment or Antigravity CLI's `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json`. For example, using the absolute host path and generated account ID:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"statusLine\": {\n    \"type\": \"command\",\n    \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/subset/apps/usage/server.mjs collect --account claude_example\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe default Claude Code account (`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, or `~/.claude` when unset) appears automatically as **Claude Code (default)** when the documented `claude auth status --json` command reports it signed in. Each refresh runs that command for every Claude account with a config directory to show its sign-in state and plan; Subset strips token variables from its environment, keeps only `loggedIn`, `authMethod`, `subscriptionType`, and `configDirectory`, and skips directories that do not exist because the CLI would create them. Removing the default account is remembered in `usage-preferences.json` next to the profile file; **Use default Claude Code account** adds it back. Quota still comes only from the status-line collector.\n\nFor several Claude Code accounts, use one [`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars) per account (for example `~/.claude` and `~/.claude-work`, launched with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-work claude`) and enter that directory when adding the account. The host stores it as `claudeConfigDir`, requires it to be unique, and shows the launch command, the `settings.json` path, and the `statusLine` JSON for that account. The collector rejects a payload whose `transcript_path` lies under a different `<config dir>/projects/` tree, falling back to `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` in its environment when no transcript path is present. Claude Code's subprocess env scrub can remove `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, so a payload with neither signal is accepted. An account without a config directory keeps the unchecked binding. The transcript path is used only for this check and is never stored.\n\nUse the generated command verbatim when paths contain spaces. This collector alone renders no status-line text. To retain an existing status line, use a wrapper that passes the same stdin JSON to the collector and existing renderer, without logging or persisting the raw payload. Subset edits provider settings only through the explicit install and uninstall operations above.\n\nThe [Claude Code status-line contract](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/statusline#rate-limit-usage) supplies `rate_limits.five_hour` and `rate_limits.seven_day`, with `used_percentage` and epoch-second `resets_at`. These subscription fields are documented for Pro/Max after the first API response. Claude Code omits a window it has no data for and drops one after it resets, so an absent window is not reported as partial data; a present window with invalid values is. The installed Claude Code CLI uses model-scoped weekly keys such as `seven_day_opus` internally, but they are not documented for the status line. The collector keeps `seven_day_fable`, `seven_day_opus`, `seven_day_sonnet`, and `seven_day_haiku` only if the status-line JSON includes them, shown as for example \"Weekly Fable usage\". Remaining quota is calculated from reported usage, not inferred from tokens or context size.\n\nThe [Antigravity CLI status-line contract](https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/statusline/) supplies `quota.<bucket>.remaining_fraction`, `reset_time`, optional `reset_in_seconds`, and optional `plan_tier`. Remaining quota is reported, while used percentage is calculated. A countdown reset is anchored to the capture time, not the dashboard refresh time.\n\nSnapshots store only allowlisted quota fields and optional plan tier. Emails, transcripts, credentials, workspace paths, and other CLI metadata are discarded. Stored envelopes bind provider and account ID; malformed, cross-account, oversized, or future-dated snapshots are rejected. A startup payload with no usable quota does not overwrite a prior observation. Refresh reloads the last snapshot without changing its capture time; snapshots become stale after five minutes. While a CLI is inactive, the dashboard cannot fetch a new observation. Removing a managed snapshot account deletes its snapshot, not the upstream sign-in; remove the old collector from CLI settings separately.\n\nUse one account-bound collector stream per snapshot slot. Snapshot replacement is atomic, but concurrent collectors for the same slot are last-completion-wins rather than capture-time ordered. Their retained observation timestamp is still shown without modification. Collection rechecks the profile after writing so an overlapping account removal does not leave a recreated managed snapshot.\n\n## Cursor team accounts\n\nSet a team API key in the environment used to launch the local host, for example under `CURSOR_WORK_TEAM_KEY`, then choose **Cursor team spending**. Enter the environment variable name, not the secret, and the exact encoded member ID (`user_...`) from the team's documented `/teams/members` operation. A second member can reference the same team credential variable; another team uses its own variable. Subset stores only references and IDs in owner-only configuration and omits both from dashboard props and profile-list responses. The source is the [official team Admin API](https://cursor.com/docs/account/teams/admin-api).\n\nThe adapter calls only the fixed `https://api.cursor.com/teams/spend` endpoint with Basic authentication and bounded pagination/response size/timeouts. It reports on-demand `spendCents`, the included usage value derived from `overallSpendCents - spendCents`, the reported enforced `effectivePerUserLimitDollars` cap, and `subscriptionCycleStart`. An absent cap or cycle end remains unknown; a spending cap is not a personal subscription quota. Authorization failures, missing members, rate limiting, and malformed responses are isolated per account, without exposing credentials or upstream response text.\n\n## Validation and release gaps\n\nRun `npm run test --workspace=@subset/usage` and `npm run test --workspace=@subset/usage-web` after the package build, alongside root `npm run check` and `npm run build`. Root checks build the catalog and usage declarations before checking consuming apps, so they do not require pre-existing ignored `dist` exports. Tests exercise synthetic multi-account provider reads, explicit access denials, safe transport failures, separate snapshot collection, normalization, redaction, profile persistence/removal, pending-login recovery, the local CLI, and host Origin checks. These are not proof of real upstream multi-account login, installed CLI compatibility, or live Cursor API authorization. No public or external-agent availability is claimed by these additions.\n\n## Superfunctions reuse review\n\nReviewed current `superfunctions-dev` source, public manifests, and README contracts on 2026-09-29, plus npm package versions. `authfn@0.3.0` is for owned session/auth flows; Codex app-server already owns this local account login. `@datafn/client@0.1.1` adds synced persistence that this slice does not need. `plugfn@0.1.0` targets third-party service connections, while this source is a local documented Codex process. `@mcpfn/core@0.0.5` is used by the isolated Outright MCP adapter; the capability itself remains host independent. `@uifn/components-svelte@0.0.1` and React equivalent were evaluated; this view currently needs only semantic HTML and a progress element, so no generic primitive dependency was added. `@superfunctions/observability@0.0.1` was evaluated; the connector avoids logging account data and has no telemetry runtime. **Package/version used in this package: none.** Gaps: hosted agent rendering, live two-account verification, and a release-ready hosted account authorization path remain.\n\nThe multi-provider extension additionally reviewed `plugfn/core` implementation, manifest and README, the provider manifest and readiness matrix, and `authfn/core` implementation, manifest and README on 2026-10-01. Published `plugfn@0.1.0` has no supported Cursor provider and requires an auth/database/encrypted persistent connection runtime; `authfn@0.3.0` owns application sessions, not externally issued team API keys or CLI snapshots. Neither is used. The local alternative remains fixed-endpoint read-only collection with environment credential references, not a competing OAuth or connection framework.\n\nThe 2026-10-08 UI update checked the current `superfunctions-dev` checkout for icon and chart packages; none is published there, so the view uses `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` and `echarts@6.1.0` directly as narrow presentation dependencies.\n\nThe package is private. A future npm release requires the `@sub-set/` scope, versioned exports, a release process, and separate verification.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c4a1d0e5-9303-4dc3-af46-1b03947f0074","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13:21:46.755Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Local sign-ins (opt-in)\n\n**Settings → Use local sign-ins** is on by default (the repository owner's choice) and stored server-side in `usage-preferences.json`; turning it off stops all reads of stored sign-ins. When on:\n\n- **Claude Code:** for each account bound to a config directory, the host first runs `claude auth status` (Claude Code refreshes its own token there), then reads the stored OAuth access token from the macOS keychain item `Claude Code-credentials` (default directory) or `Claude Code-credentials-<first 8 hex of SHA-256 of the directory>`, falling back to `<dir>/.credentials.json`. It calls `https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage` read-only and shows `five_hour`, `seven_day`, and `limits[]` entries of kind `weekly_scoped` (per-model weekly limits such as Fable). Subset never calls the token endpoint: Anthropic rotates refresh tokens, so refreshing would sign Claude Code out. An expired token asks the user to open Claude Code. If no stored sign-in is found, the status-line snapshot is used.\n- **Factory Droid:** reads `<factory home>/.factory/auth.v2.loginkeychain`, `auth.v2.keyring`, or `auth.v2.file`, decrypts it (AES-256-GCM) with the key from the keychain item `Factory CLI` or `auth.v2.key`, and calls `https://api.factory.ai/api/billing/limits` (or the EU host) with the account's organization. When off, a Droid account needs a Factory API key variable; Subset then calls `/api/cli/whoami` for the organization and the same limits endpoint.\n- **Codex** is unchanged: Codex app-server already reads its own sign-in through a documented interface.\n\nNeither endpoint is documented by its provider, so either can change or stop working. Tokens stay in memory for one request and are never stored, logged, or sent to the view. macOS may show a keychain prompt the first time; choose **Always Allow** so auto-refresh does not wait on it. The approach follows Synara (MIT; `apps/server/src/providerUsage/providers`), Orca, and Zeron, which read the same stores. Subset's implementation is independent and read-only. Keychain lookup is macOS-only; elsewhere only credential files are read.\n\n## Standalone local preview\n\nIn the local dashboard, choose **Use current Codex account** to add the existing ChatGPT sign-in, then enter a label and choose **Connect another ChatGPT account**. The host starts the [documented Codex app-server browser login](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app-server) in a new, isolated Codex home. Follow **Continue with ChatGPT** and choose the intended account in the browser. The dashboard polls the login result and adds the account after Codex confirms a ChatGPT sign-in. A browser reload recovers the active sign-in ID, validated link, and state from the same local server, restoring progress and cancellation controls without persisting credentials or login state in browser storage. This recovery does not survive a server restart. Subset never sees or stores OAuth tokens; Codex manages them in its own home. **Remove** unlinks an existing Codex home from the dashboard; for a Subset-managed home it also deletes that local sign-in after explicit confirmation. Cancelling an unfinished login deletes only its newly created home.\n\nThe host stores profile labels and Codex home paths in `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` with owner-only file permissions. Managed Codex homes live under `~/.local/share/subset/codex-accounts/`, each with owner-only directory permissions. An owner-controlled profile file may also be supplied directly, for example:\n\n```json\n[\n  { \"id\": \"personal\", \"label\": \"Personal\", \"codexHome\": \"/absolute/path/to/personal-codex-home\" },\n  { \"id\": \"work\", \"label\": \"Work\", \"codexHome\": \"/absolute/path/to/work-codex-home\" }\n]\n```\n\nEach manually added home must already be signed in with its intended ChatGPT account using Codex. `CODEX_HOME=/absolute/path/to/personal-codex-home codex login` is the Codex-managed login path. `--profile` selects a config overlay within one home and is not account isolation. Do not copy auth files between homes. Account email is not stored in profile metadata or sent to the dashboard status view, so labels should distinguish accounts. A second real ChatGPT sign-in has not yet been available for live two-account verification.\n\nAfter `npm run build`, use `npm run status --workspace=@subset/usage-web` for structured JSON or `npm run serve --workspace=@subset/usage-web` for the local view at `http://127.0.0.1:4174`. `SUBSET_USAGE_PROFILES_FILE` selects a different JSON file; the existing `SUBSET_CODEX_PROFILES_FILE` remains a fallback for persisted Codex setups. The default stays `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` and accepts all provider types. `SUBSET_USAGE_DATA_DIR` optionally selects a managed data directory; `SUBSET_USAGE_PORT` changes the loopback port. Stop the server with Ctrl-C or SIGTERM. The server binds only to `127.0.0.1`, checks Host and Origin headers, and requires a matching browser Origin for account mutations. This is a local preview, not a published CLI/skill, hosted agent view, or public web app. OpenAI documents Codex app-server authentication for local or open-source applications, with a different authorization path required for commercial or hosted services.\n\n## Claude and Antigravity collectors\n\nIn **Manage accounts**, choose **Claude Code quota snapshot** or **Antigravity CLI quota snapshot**, give the account a distinct label, and choose **Add snapshot account**. Expand that account's collector setup and use its generated command, which includes its unique local ID and any custom profile file selection. The command reads JSON on stdin, writes an owner-only quota snapshot, and deliberately produces no stdout.\n\n**Automatic installation.** For a Claude Code account bound to a config directory, or an Antigravity account, **Install collector** (on the card, in account settings, or as a checkbox when adding the account) writes the collector into that CLI's `settings.json`. It is an explicit operation: Subset first copies the file to `settings.json.subset-backup`, keeps every other key, and saves the replaced `statusLine` in an owner-only Subset file. The collector then runs that previous status line with the same input, so an existing display (for example Orca's) keeps rendering. **Uninstall** or removing the account restores the previous `statusLine` unless another tool has since replaced it. Status-line collectors only run in CLI sessions started after installation; a session that was already open keeps its old settings. Account settings report whether the collector is installed, missing, or replaced by another tool. Each collector run records only its time and outcome (`saved`, `no-quota`, or `wrong-directory`) in an owner-only `collector-runs` directory, so the dashboard can say whether the collector has not run yet (for example, a CLI session opened before installation), ran without quota, or ran from the wrong config directory. Settings that are not valid JSON are never rewritten. An account without a config directory cannot be installed automatically.\n\nFor manual setup, configure that command as `statusLine.command` in the intended Claude Code account environment or Antigravity CLI's `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json`. For example, using the absolute host path and generated account ID:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"statusLine\": {\n    \"type\": \"command\",\n    \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/subset/apps/usage/server.mjs collect --account claude_example\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe default Claude Code account (`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, or `~/.claude` when unset) appears automatically as **Claude Code (default)** when the documented `claude auth status --json` command reports it signed in. Each refresh runs that command for every Claude account with a config directory to show its sign-in state and plan; Subset strips token variables from its environment, keeps only `loggedIn`, `authMethod`, `subscriptionType`, and `configDirectory`, and skips directories that do not exist because the CLI would create them. Removing the default account is remembered in `usage-preferences.json` next to the profile file; **Use default Claude Code account** adds it back. Quota still comes only from the status-line collector.\n\nFor several Claude Code accounts, use one [`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars) per account (for example `~/.claude` and `~/.claude-work`, launched with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-work claude`) and enter that directory when adding the account. The host stores it as `claudeConfigDir`, requires it to be unique, and shows the launch command, the `settings.json` path, and the `statusLine` JSON for that account. The collector rejects a payload whose `transcript_path` lies under a different `<config dir>/projects/` tree, falling back to `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` in its environment when no transcript path is present. Claude Code's subprocess env scrub can remove `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, so a payload with neither signal is accepted. An account without a config directory keeps the unchecked binding. The transcript path is used only for this check and is never stored.\n\nUse the generated command verbatim when paths contain spaces. This collector alone renders no status-line text. To retain an existing status line, use a wrapper that passes the same stdin JSON to the collector and existing renderer, without logging or persisting the raw payload. Subset edits provider settings only through the explicit install and uninstall operations above.\n\nThe [Claude Code status-line contract](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/statusline#rate-limit-usage) supplies `rate_limits.five_hour` and `rate_limits.seven_day`, with `used_percentage` and epoch-second `resets_at`. These subscription fields are documented for Pro/Max after the first API response. Claude Code omits a window it has no data for and drops one after it resets, so an absent window is not reported as partial data; a present window with invalid values is. The installed Claude Code CLI uses model-scoped weekly keys such as `seven_day_opus` internally, but they are not documented for the status line. The collector keeps `seven_day_fable`, `seven_day_opus`, `seven_day_sonnet`, and `seven_day_haiku` only if the status-line JSON includes them, shown as for example \"Weekly Fable usage\". Remaining quota is calculated from reported usage, not inferred from tokens or context size.\n\nThe [Antigravity CLI status-line contract](https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/statusline/) supplies `quota.<bucket>.remaining_fraction`, `reset_time`, optional `reset_in_seconds`, and optional `plan_tier`. Remaining quota is reported, while used percentage is calculated. A countdown reset is anchored to the capture time, not the dashboard refresh time.\n\nSnapshots store only allowlisted quota fields and optional plan tier. Emails, transcripts, credentials, workspace paths, and other CLI metadata are discarded. Stored envelopes bind provider and account ID; malformed, cross-account, oversized, or future-dated snapshots are rejected. A startup payload with no usable quota does not overwrite a prior observation. Refresh reloads the last snapshot without changing its capture time; snapshots become stale after five minutes. While a CLI is inactive, the dashboard cannot fetch a new observation. Removing a managed snapshot account deletes its snapshot, not the upstream sign-in; remove the old collector from CLI settings separately.\n\nUse one account-bound collector stream per snapshot slot. Snapshot replacement is atomic, but concurrent collectors for the same slot are last-completion-wins rather than capture-time ordered. Their retained observation timestamp is still shown without modification. Collection rechecks the profile after writing so an overlapping account removal does not leave a recreated managed snapshot.\n\n## Cursor team accounts\n\nSet a team API key in the environment used to launch the local host, for example under `CURSOR_WORK_TEAM_KEY`, then choose **Cursor team spending**. Enter the environment variable name, not the secret, and the exact encoded member ID (`user_...`) from the team's documented `/teams/members` operation. A second member can reference the same team credential variable; another team uses its own variable. Subset stores only references and IDs in owner-only configuration and omits both from dashboard props and profile-list responses. The source is the [official team Admin API](https://cursor.com/docs/account/teams/admin-api).\n\nThe adapter calls only the fixed `https://api.cursor.com/teams/spend` endpoint with Basic authentication and bounded pagination/response size/timeouts. It reports on-demand `spendCents`, the included usage value derived from `overallSpendCents - spendCents`, the reported enforced `effectivePerUserLimitDollars` cap, and `subscriptionCycleStart`. An absent cap or cycle end remains unknown; a spending cap is not a personal subscription quota. Authorization failures, missing members, rate limiting, and malformed responses are isolated per account, without exposing credentials or upstream response text.\n\n## Validation and release gaps\n\nRun `npm run test --workspace=@subset/usage` and `npm run test --workspace=@subset/usage-web` after the package build, alongside root `npm run check` and `npm run build`. Root checks build the catalog and usage declarations before checking consuming apps, so they do not require pre-existing ignored `dist` exports. Tests exercise synthetic multi-account provider reads, explicit access denials, safe transport failures, separate snapshot collection, normalization, redaction, profile persistence/removal, pending-login recovery, the local CLI, and host Origin checks. These are not proof of real upstream multi-account login, installed CLI compatibility, or live Cursor API authorization. No public or external-agent availability is claimed by these additions.\n\n## Superfunctions reuse review\n\nReviewed current `superfunctions-dev` source, public manifests, and README contracts on 2026-09-29, plus npm package versions. `authfn@0.3.0` is for owned session/auth flows; Codex app-server already owns this local account login. `@datafn/client@0.1.1` adds synced persistence that this slice does not need. `plugfn@0.1.0` targets third-party service connections, while this source is a local documented Codex process. `@mcpfn/core@0.0.5` is used by the isolated Outright MCP adapter; the capability itself remains host independent. `@uifn/components-svelte@0.0.1` and React equivalent were evaluated; this view currently needs only semantic HTML and a progress element, so no generic primitive dependency was added. `@superfunctions/observability@0.0.1` was evaluated; the connector avoids logging account data and has no telemetry runtime. **Package/version used in this package: none.** Gaps: hosted agent rendering, live two-account verification, and a release-ready hosted account authorization path remain.\n\nThe multi-provider extension additionally reviewed `plugfn/core` implementation, manifest and README, the provider manifest and readiness matrix, and `authfn/core` implementation, manifest and README on 2026-10-01. Published `plugfn@0.1.0` has no supported Cursor provider and requires an auth/database/encrypted persistent connection runtime; `authfn@0.3.0` owns application sessions, not externally issued team API keys or CLI snapshots. Neither is used. The local alternative remains fixed-endpoint read-only collection with environment credential references, not a competing OAuth or connection framework.\n\nThe 2026-10-08 UI update checked the current `superfunctions-dev` checkout for icon and chart packages; none is published there, so the view uses `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` and `echarts@6.1.0` directly as narrow presentation dependencies.\n\nThe package is private. A future npm release requires the `@sub-set/` scope, versioned exports, a release process, and separate verification.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"eafa5d8e-0278-4909-be9a-5cf136d0d8e7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
