4:58.651Z","session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"76f0f542-d645-457b-bc4b-16ce0b448b73","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"54454a4d-12b9-4ee3-b2a6-9023849a1ade","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WKAReLL1psR7FQ5b4YabMz","type":"tool_result","content":".successful = True\n.data.context = None\n.data.files[0].byteSize = 6236\n.data.files[0].mediaType = text/markdown; charset=utf-8\n.data.files[0].path = SKILL.md\n.data.files[0].sha256 = 37a7c581941ed581073870f95de13cd6fd39ce6e3703b1cef101be0c3a5a53c7\n.data.files[1].byteSize = 570\n.data.files[1].mediaType = application/json\n.data.files[1].path = skill.json\n.data.files[1].sha256 = b369998554caece8ae0824e23e448cf07b0a19c5dd1f97455b029af0f1b1440f\n== .data.instructions\n# Linear Backlog Architect\n\nShape a product outcome or an existing Linear backlog into executable, reviewable issues for an agent implementation loop. Use this skill when asked to decompose a feature, audit issue scope, sequence sub-issues, or prepare issues for automated delivery. For an idea that only needs durable capture and product/architecture documents, use `21n/add-to-backlog` instead.\n\n## Modes and authority\n\n- **Plan or audit:** read the relevant repository, architecture, issue descriptions/discussion, existing backlog, and loop configuration. Return a proposed issue tree, dependency graph, and readiness findings without changing Linear or the execution queue.\n- **Publish:** when the user has asked to create or revise issues, make the approved outcome concrete in Linear. This request authorizes the corresponding issue, document, and relation writes; do not ask again for routine details that are supported by the project context. If the destination or a product decision needed to write truthful acceptance criteria remains ambiguous, ask only about that decision.\n- **Queue:** update a delivery loop's ordered issue/dependency configuration only when the user has also authorized adding this work to that loop. Publishing issues does not itself start implementation, dispatch cards, merge PRs, or mark issues Done.\n\nUse Composio CLI for all Linear reads and writes in the `21n` workspace, with the configured account when selecting Linear tools. Before issue creation or document association, retrieve the published `21n/create-and-associate-linear-issue@1.0.0` skill and follow its destination, duplicate, partial-write, and read-back rules. Use the relevant Linear relation tools for parent/child and blocked-by links, then read them back. After an ambiguous write response, inspect Linear before retrying; never create a second issue to repair an uncertain first write.\n\n## Build the delivery shape\n\n1. Establish the outcome, current architecture and repository boundaries, decisions already made, and evidence that will establish completion. Distinguish facts, proposals, assumptions, and unresolved product choices. Preserve existing issue IDs, discussion, links, and any active PR scope.\n2. Use a parent issue for the outcome when several PR-sized pieces belong together. The parent describes the integrated result and completion gate; executable children describe one coherent behavior or invariant each. Do not dispatch an umbrella issue as if it were another implementation slice. For a genuinely single-PR outcome, one issue is enough.\n3. Split where pieces have different behavior, subsystem ownership, validation, release risk, or review context. Keep a tightly coupled invariant together when splitting would leave an invalid intermediate state. Do not split by arbitrary file or line counts, and do not create bookkeeping children that cannot be independently verified.\n4. For every executable issue, write a compact contract: user/system trigger and expected behavior; scope and explicit exclusions; owned boundary and relevant interfaces/invariants; concrete acceptance checks with observable evidence; verification surface (unit, integration, UI, CI, external/manual); dependencies; and any migration, rollback, or rollout gate that actually applies. Name a deferred manual acceptance issue explicitly rather than treating unrun manual testing as proof.\n5. Draw a directed acyclic graph of **real prerequisites**. Use Linear blocked-by relations for required ordering; parent/child links are hierarchy, not dependency. Distinguish a preferred execution order from a blocker. Parallel children need stable shared interfaces, compatible base branches, and a stated integration point. If stacked PRs are necessary, identify the base and the merge/rebase sequence.\n6. Carry cross-cutting requirements into each affected child's acceptance criteria and, where needed, a final integrated acceptance issue. Do not put all implementation into one broad cross-cutting ticket.\n\n## Readiness decision\n\nBefore publishing or queueing, assess every executable issue for: a bounded, coherent PR outcome; known prerequisite contract; observable acceptance evidence; explicit deferred/external validation; no unresolved decision that would change the implementation contract; and compatibility with the current repository and loop. Mark it `ready`, `needs decision`, or `needs split`, with the reason. A parent outcome is `container`, not an executable `ready` issue. A large issue may be ready if it is one atomic invariant and the reviewer can assess it as one change.\n\nReturn an ordered table with proposed or existing issue IDs, parent, outcome, dependencies, parallelism, readiness, and verification. Include the smallest useful issue descriptions, a dependency DAG, and any decision required from the user. For an existing backlog, propose changes only to unstarted issues by default; report active PR work separately rather than rewriting its acceptance contract mid-review.\n\n## Publishing and verification\n\nSearch the chosen project/team for equivalent issues first. Preserve a supplied existing issue and its discussion; if a likely duplicate is found for a new issue, resolve reuse versus distinct creation with the user as the shared Linear skill requires. Create or amend only the issues in scope, then add parent/child and blocked-by relations. Confirm each issue's ID, URL, team, project, state, description, parent, dependencies, and required links by reading Linear back. Check the team setting for parent/sub-issue auto-close before relying on a manual completion gate; if auto-close could mark a parent Done without the owner's go, report that conflict and do not claim the gate is protected.\n\nFor the shared Hermes Linear loop at `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/automation/linear-loop`, queue only executable ready issues, keep the existing config's order/dependency conventions, run its config and focused tests, and dry-run the coordinator. Preserve existing dirty work and active cards. Do not start the coordinator or dispatch new work unless that action was requested. Report the published IDs, verified relations, queue changes, tests, and any remaining unverified external behavior.\n\n.data.requestId = mcp:41e4878f-ff98-40a7-b11d-7ce6e5180d82\n== .data.skill.description\nDesign, audit, and publish PR-sized Linear implementation issues with explicit dependencies and acceptance evidence before agent-loop intake; use for backlog decomposition rather than single-idea capture.\n.data.skill.id = skill:c75f8956-ca80-4594-a3af-69488d839b9b\n.data.skill.name = Linear Backlog Architect\n.data.skill.slug = linear-backlog-architect\n.data.skill.tags[0] = backlog\n.data.skill.tags[1] = implementation\n.data.skill.tags[2] = linear\n.data.skill.tags[3] = planning\n.data.skill.tags[4] = reviewability\n.data.skill.visibility = workspace\n.data.skill.workspaceId = workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\n.data.skill.workspaceSlug = 21n\n.data.version.byteSize = 3348\n.data.version.createdAt = 2026-09-25T10:29:17.432Z\n.data.version.digest = sha256:5d0c2a41815db6c165bd163155fdce6d51cdff14d79f3c0f5feb4940935890d6\n.data.version.id = skill-version:5417d966-3f24-44a2-90e5-1a60f22afb4e\n.data.version.manifest.byteSize = 3348\n.data.version.manifest.digest = sha256:5d0c2a41815db6c165bd163155fdce6d51cdff14d79f3c0f5feb4940935890d6\n.data.version.manifest.expandedByteSize = 6806\n.data.version.manifest.fileCount = 2\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].byteSize = 6236\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].mediaType = text/markdown; charset=utf-8\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].path = SKILL.md\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].sha256 = 37a7c581941ed581073870f95de13cd6fd39ce6e3703b1cef101be0c3a5a53c7\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].byteSize = 570\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].mediaType = application/json\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].path = skill.json\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].sha256 = b369998554caece8ae0824e23e448cf07b0a19c5dd1f97455b029af0f1b1440f\n.data.version.manifest.formatVersion = 1\n.data.version.publishedAt = 2026-09-25T10:29:17.432Z\n.data.version.revision = 1\n.data.version.semanticVersion = 1.0.0\n.data.version.state = published\n.error = None\n.logId = log_t_sH1Wz8RSso\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b2f82d6d-d692-4b15-acae-242254dd587a","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13:24:58.839Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":".successful = True\n.data.context = None\n.data.files[0].byteSize = 6236\n.data.files[0].mediaType = text/markdown; charset=utf-8\n.data.files[0].path = SKILL.md\n.data.files[0].sha256 = 37a7c581941ed581073870f95de13cd6fd39ce6e3703b1cef101be0c3a5a53c7\n.data.files[1].byteSize = 570\n.data.files[1].mediaType = application/json\n.data.files[1].path = skill.json\n.data.files[1].sha256 = b369998554caece8ae0824e23e448cf07b0a19c5dd1f97455b029af0f1b1440f\n== .data.instructions\n# Linear Backlog Architect\n\nShape a product outcome or an existing Linear backlog into executable, reviewable issues for an agent implementation loop. Use this skill when asked to decompose a feature, audit issue scope, sequence sub-issues, or prepare issues for automated delivery. For an idea that only needs durable capture and product/architecture documents, use `21n/add-to-backlog` instead.\n\n## Modes and authority\n\n- **Plan or audit:** read the relevant repository, architecture, issue descriptions/discussion, existing backlog, and loop configuration. Return a proposed issue tree, dependency graph, and readiness findings without changing Linear or the execution queue.\n- **Publish:** when the user has asked to create or revise issues, make the approved outcome concrete in Linear. This request authorizes the corresponding issue, document, and relation writes; do not ask again for routine details that are supported by the project context. If the destination or a product decision needed to write truthful acceptance criteria remains ambiguous, ask only about that decision.\n- **Queue:** update a delivery loop's ordered issue/dependency configuration only when the user has also authorized adding this work to that loop. Publishing issues does not itself start implementation, dispatch cards, merge PRs, or mark issues Done.\n\nUse Composio CLI for all Linear reads and writes in the `21n` workspace, with the configured account when selecting Linear tools. Before issue creation or document association, retrieve the published `21n/create-and-associate-linear-issue@1.0.0` skill and follow its destination, duplicate, partial-write, and read-back rules. Use the relevant Linear relation tools for parent/child and blocked-by links, then read them back. After an ambiguous write response, inspect Linear before retrying; never create a second issue to repair an uncertain first write.\n\n## Build the delivery shape\n\n1. Establish the outcome, current architecture and repository boundaries, decisions already made, and evidence that will establish completion. Distinguish facts, proposals, assumptions, and unresolved product choices. Preserve existing issue IDs, discussion, links, and any active PR scope.\n2. Use a parent issue for the outcome when several PR-sized pieces belong together. The parent describes the integrated result and completion gate; executable children describe one coherent behavior or invariant each. Do not dispatch an umbrella issue as if it were another implementation slice. For a genuinely single-PR outcome, one issue is enough.\n3. Split where pieces have different behavior, subsystem ownership, validation, release risk, or review context. Keep a tightly coupled invariant together when splitting would leave an invalid intermediate state. Do not split by arbitrary file or line counts, and do not create bookkeeping children that cannot be independently verified.\n4. For every executable issue, write a compact contract: user/system trigger and expected behavior; scope and explicit exclusions; owned boundary and relevant interfaces/invariants; concrete acceptance checks with observable evidence; verification surface (unit, integration, UI, CI, external/manual); dependencies; and any migration, rollback, or rollout gate that actually applies. Name a deferred manual acceptance issue explicitly rather than treating unrun manual testing as proof.\n5. Draw a directed acyclic graph of **real prerequisites**. Use Linear blocked-by relations for required ordering; parent/child links are hierarchy, not dependency. Distinguish a preferred execution order from a blocker. Parallel children need stable shared interfaces, compatible base branches, and a stated integration point. If stacked PRs are necessary, identify the base and the merge/rebase sequence.\n6. Carry cross-cutting requirements into each affected child's acceptance criteria and, where needed, a final integrated acceptance issue. Do not put all implementation into one broad cross-cutting ticket.\n\n## Readiness decision\n\nBefore publishing or queueing, assess every executable issue for: a bounded, coherent PR outcome; known prerequisite contract; observable acceptance evidence; explicit deferred/external validation; no unresolved decision that would change the implementation contract; and compatibility with the current repository and loop. Mark it `ready`, `needs decision`, or `needs split`, with the reason. A parent outcome is `container`, not an executable `ready` issue. A large issue may be ready if it is one atomic invariant and the reviewer can assess it as one change.\n\nReturn an ordered table with proposed or existing issue IDs, parent, outcome, dependencies, parallelism, readiness, and verification. Include the smallest useful issue descriptions, a dependency DAG, and any decision required from the user. For an existing backlog, propose changes only to unstarted issues by default; report active PR work separately rather than rewriting its acceptance contract mid-review.\n\n## Publishing and verification\n\nSearch the chosen project/team for equivalent issues first. Preserve a supplied existing issue and its discussion; if a likely duplicate is found for a new issue, resolve reuse versus distinct creation with the user as the shared Linear skill requires. Create or amend only the issues in scope, then add parent/child and blocked-by relations. Confirm each issue's ID, URL, team, project, state, description, parent, dependencies, and required links by reading Linear back. Check the team setting for parent/sub-issue auto-close before relying on a manual completion gate; if auto-close could mark a parent Done without the owner's go, report that conflict and do not claim the gate is protected.\n\nFor the shared Hermes Linear loop at `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/automation/linear-loop`, queue only executable ready issues, keep the existing config's order/dependency conventions, run its config and focused tests, and dry-run the coordinator. Preserve existing dirty work and active cards. Do not start the coordinator or dispatch new work unless that action was requested. Report the published IDs, verified relations, queue changes, tests, and any remaining unverified external behavior.\n\n.data.requestId = mcp:41e4878f-ff98-40a7-b11d-7ce6e5180d82\n== .data.skill.description\nDesign, audit, and publish PR-sized Linear implementation issues with explicit dependencies and acceptance evidence before agent-loop intake; use for backlog decomposition rather than single-idea capture.\n.data.skill.id = skill:c75f8956-ca80-4594-a3af-69488d839b9b\n.data.skill.name = Linear Backlog Architect\n.data.skill.slug = linear-backlog-architect\n.data.skill.tags[0] = backlog\n.data.skill.tags[1] = implementation\n.data.skill.tags[2] = linear\n.data.skill.tags[3] = planning\n.data.skill.tags[4] = reviewability\n.data.skill.visibility = workspace\n.data.skill.workspaceId = workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\n.data.skill.workspaceSlug = 21n\n.data.version.byteSize = 3348\n.data.version.createdAt = 2026-09-25T10:29:17.432Z\n.data.version.digest = sha256:5d0c2a41815db6c165bd163155fdce6d51cdff14d79f3c0f5feb4940935890d6\n.data.version.id = skill-version:5417d966-3f24-44a2-90e5-1a60f22afb4e\n.data.version.manifest.byteSize = 3348\n.data.version.manifest.digest = sha256:5d0c2a41815db6c165bd163155fdce6d51cdff14d79f3c0f5feb4940935890d6\n.data.version.manifest.expandedByteSize = 6806\n.data.version.manifest.fileCount = 2\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].byteSize = 6236\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].mediaType = text/markdown; charset=utf-8\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].path = SKILL.md\n.data.version.manifest.files[0].sha256 = 37a7c581941ed581073870f95de13cd6fd39ce6e3703b1cef101be0c3a5a53c7\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].byteSize = 570\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].mediaType = application/json\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].path = skill.json\n.data.version.manifest.files[1].sha256 = b369998554caece8ae0824e23e448cf07b0a19c5dd1f97455b029af0f1b1440f\n.data.version.manifest.formatVersion = 1\n.data.version.publishedAt = 2026-09-25T10:29:17.432Z\n.data.version.revision = 1\n.data.version.semanticVersion = 1.0.0\n.data.version.state = published\n.error = None\n.logId = log_t_sH1Wz8RSso","stderr":"\nShell c