2:05.247Z","session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"08ebbb2b-c9ed-440f-a40d-d8d40ac8a9de","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6d43e326-7ee1-41e3-ad41-37cb97bf8598","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014USPG1LJ9qGeiHMNQ5buEx","type":"tool_result","content":"1791467528937 COMPLETED 240 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n1791467530584 COMPLETED 137 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n# pass 93\n# fail 0\n# pass 36\n# fail 0","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8fe9bd54-b9c6-4681-be42-0b45e6cdeebe","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13:52:18.962Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1791467528937 COMPLETED 240 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n1791467530584 COMPLETED 137 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n# pass 93\n# fail 0\n# pass 36\n# fail 0","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage-cli/README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":18,"oldLines":14,"newStart":18,"newLines":14,"lines":[" | Command | What it does |"," | --- | --- |"," | `serve` (default) | Starts the dashboard on `127.0.0.1` (port 4174, or the next free port) and opens it. `--port <n>` requires that port; `--no-open` skips the browser. Ctrl-C stops it. |","-| `status` | Prints the normalized usage status (`@subset/usage` contract, schema version 1) and exits. |","+| `status` | Prints the normalized usage status (`@subset/usage` contract, schema version 1) and exits. It writes nothing: a signed-in default Claude account is shown without being saved, and no history is recorded. |"," | `collect --account <id>` | Saves a quota snapshot from a Claude Code or Antigravity status line. Installed collectors call it through a shim; you rarely run it yourself. |"," | `--help`, `--version` | |"," "," ## Data and permissions"," "," - Configuration: `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` (override with `SUBSET_USAGE_PROFILES_FILE`).","-- Data: `~/.local/share/subset/` (override with `SUBSET_USAGE_DATA_DIR`): managed Codex homes, quota snapshots, usage history (times and percentages only), and the collector shim `bin/subset-usage-collect`.","+- Data: `~/.local/share/subset/` (override with `SUBSET_USAGE_DATA_DIR`): managed Codex homes, quota snapshots, usage history (times and percentages only), and the collector shim `bin/subset-usage-collect` (`bin/subset-usage-collect-<hash>` for a custom profiles file, so configurations sharing a data directory keep separate collectors)."," - **Use local sign-ins** is off until you choose on the dashboard's first-run question. When on, the CLI reads, without changing or refreshing, the sign-ins Claude Code, Factory Droid, the Cursor app, Devin, Pi, OpenCode, omp, and Hermes keep on this computer, and calls the usage endpoints those tools use. Those endpoints are undocumented and may change. Tokens are never stored, logged, or sent to the page."," - Installing a collector edits that CLI's `settings.json` only when you ask, keeps a backup, and keeps your previous status line running."," - The local API accepts only same-origin requests carrying the dashboard's own header."]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage/README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":6,"newStart":28,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," - **Claude Code:** for each account bound to a config directory, the host first runs `claude auth status` (Claude Code refreshes its own token there), then reads the stored OAuth access token from the macOS keychain item `Claude Code-credentials` (default directory) or `Claude Code-credentials-<first 8 hex of SHA-256 of the directory>`, falling back to `<dir>/.credentials.json`. It calls `https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage` read-only and shows `five_hour`, `seven_day`, and `limits[]` entries of kind `weekly_scoped` (per-model weekly limits such as Fable). Subset never calls the token endpoint: Anthropic rotates refresh tokens, so refreshing would sign Claude Code out. An expired token asks the user to open Claude Code. If no stored sign-in is found, the status-line snapshot is used."," - **Factory Droid:** reads `<factory home>/.factory/auth.v2.loginkeychain`, `auth.v2.keyring`, or `auth.v2.file`, decrypts it (AES-256-GCM) with the key from the keychain item `Factory CLI` or `auth.v2.key`, and calls `https://api.factory.ai/api/billing/limits` (or the EU host) with the account's organization. When off, a Droid account needs a Factory API key variable; Subset then calls `/api/cli/whoami` for the organization and the same limits endpoint.","+- **Configured keys win:** a Factory Droid or Devin account set up with an API key variable always reads that key's account and reports an error when the variable is unset, rather than falling back to the default local sign-in.","+- **Harness logins** (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) are grouped by email and ChatGPT workspace, and join a direct ChatGPT account only when the plans agree, so a Personal and a Team workspace under one email stay separate. Cached reads are keyed by a hash of the stored login, so a replaced login is read again at once."," - **Codex** is unchanged: Codex app-server already reads its own sign-in through a documented interface."," "," Neither endpoint is documented by its provider, so either can change or stop working. Tokens stay in memory for one request and are never stored, logged, or sent to the view. macOS may show a keychain prompt the first time; choose **Always Allow** so auto-refresh does not wait on it. The approach follows Synara (MIT; `apps/server/src/providerUsage/providers`), Orca, and Zeron, which read the same stores. Subset's implementation is independent and read-only. Keychain lookup is macOS-only; elsewhere only credential files are read."]},{"oldStart":94,"oldLines":7,"newStart":96,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," For several Claude Code accounts, use one [`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars) per account (for example `~/.claude` and `~/.claude-work`, launched with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-work claude`) and enter that directory when adding the account. The host stores it as `claudeConfigDir`, requires it to be unique, and shows the launch command, the `settings.json` path, and the `statusLine` JSON for that account. The collector rejects a payload whose `transcript_path` lies under a different `<config dir>/projects/` tree, falling back to `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` in its environment when no transcript path is present. Claude Code's subprocess env scrub can remove `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, so a payload with neither signal is accepted. An account without a config directory keeps the unchecked binding. The transcript path is used only for this check and is never stored."," ","-Collectors call a shim, `<data dir>/bin/subset-usage-collect` (default `~/.local/share/subset/bin`), that `subset-usage serve` rewrites on each start to point at the current CLI, so upgrading or moving the install does not break installed status lines. On start, the server also rewrites collectors installed with an older command, but only where the settings still hold the exact recorded collector. When one collector replaces another account's collector in a shared settings file (Antigravity), it inherits the status line that collector had replaced, and `settings.json.subset-backup` keeps the first pre-collector copy. Use the generated command verbatim when paths contain spaces. This collector alone renders no status-line text. To retain an existing status line, use a wrapper that passes the same stdin JSON to the collector and existing renderer, without logging or persisting the raw payload. Subset edits provider settings only through the explicit install and uninstall operations above.","+Collectors call a shim, `<data dir>/bin/subset-usage-collect` (default `~/.local/share/subset/bin`), that `subset-usage serve` rewrites on each start to point at the current CLI, so upgrading or moving the install does not break installed status lines. On start, the server also rewrites collectors installed with an older command, but only where the settings still hold the exact recorded collector. When one collector replaces another account's collector in a shared settings file (Antigravity), it inherits the status line that collector had replaced, and `settings.json.subset-backup` keeps the first pre-collector copy. Antigravity keeps one `settings.json`, so only one Antigravity account can collect at a time: installing for a second account is refused while another account's collector is installed there, and that account's settings say which account holds it. Claude snapshots carry no identity, so the host records the email `claude auth status` reports for each account (`claude-identities.json` in the data directory); after a sign-in change, snapshots observed earlier are not shown under the new account. Use the generated command verbatim when paths contain spaces. This collector alone renders no status-line text. To retain an existing status line, use a wrapper that passes the same stdin JSON to the collector and existing renderer, without logging or persisting the raw payload. Subset edits provider settings only through the explicit install and uninstall operations above."," "," The [Claude Code status-line contract](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/statusline#rate-limit-usage) supplies `rate_limits.five_hour` and `rate_limits.seven_day`, with `used_percentage` and epoch-second `resets_at`. These subscription fields are documented for Pro/Max after the first API response. Claude Code omits a window it has no data for and drops one after it resets, so an absent window is not reported as partial data; a present window with invalid values is. The installed Claude Code CLI uses model-scoped weekly keys such as `seven_day_opus` internally, but they are not documented for the status line. The collector keeps `seven_day_fable`, `seven_day_opus`, `seven_day_sonnet`, and `seven_day_haiku` only if the status-line JSON includes them, shown as for example \"Weekly Fable usage\". Remaining quota is calculated from reported usage, not inferred from tokens or context size."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage-cli/README.md","/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage/README.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"08ebbb2b-c9ed-440f-a40d-d8d40ac8a9de","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"main","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
