6:57.301Z","session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"usage-dashboard-cli","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"7c97b241-b9e2-4041-8618-2710891c2b95","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c774b04c-c23f-4473-b206-32f54e0c9d68","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012qd116E56EU1vNXeusH7ki","type":"tool_result","content":"15:| Amp | Credit balance and daily Amp Free allowance from the documented `amp usage` command | The CLI's signed-in account, or another account's `AMP_API_KEY` named by an environment variable |\n19:| Claude Code (live, opt-in) | 5-hour, weekly, and per-model weekly limits (for example Fable) from Claude Code's undocumented `/api/oauth/usage` | The sign-in stored for each bound `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`; falls back to the status-line snapshot |\n55:The host stores profile labels and Codex home paths in `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` with owner-only file permissions. Managed Codex homes live under `~/.local/share/subset/codex-accounts/`, each with owner-only directory permissions. An owner-controlled profile file may also be supplied directly, for example:\n74:The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.\n82:**Automatic installation.** For a Claude Code account bound to a config directory, or an Antigravity account, **Install collector** (on the card, in account settings, or as a checkbox when adding the account) writes the collector into that CLI's `settings.json`. It is an explicit operation: Subset first copies the file to `settings.json.subset-backup`, keeps every other key, and saves the replaced `statusLine` in an owner-only Subset file. The collector then runs that previous status line with the same input, so an existing display (for example Orca's) keeps rendering. **Uninstall** or removing the account restores the previous `statusLine` unless another tool has since replaced it. Status-line collectors only run in CLI sessions started after installation; a session that was already open keeps its old settings. Account settings report whether the collector is installed, missing, or replaced by another tool. Each collector run records only its time and outcome (`saved`, `no-quota`, or `wrong-directory`) in an owner-only `collector-runs` directory, so the dashboard can say whether the collector has not run yet (for example, a CLI session opened before installation), ran without quota, or ran from the wrong config directory. Settings that are not valid JSON are never rewritten. An account without a config directory cannot be installed automatically.\n84:For manual setup, configure that command as `statusLine.command` in the intended Claude Code account environment or Antigravity CLI's `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json`. For example, using the absolute host path and generated account ID:\n90:    \"command\": \"'/Users/you/.local/share/subset/bin/subset-usage-collect' --account 'claude_example'\"\n97:For several Claude Code accounts, use one [`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars) per account (for example `~/.claude` and `~/.claude-work`, launched with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-work claude`) and enter that directory when adding the account. The host stores it as `claudeConfigDir`, requires it to be unique, and shows the launch command, the `settings.json` path, and the `statusLine` JSON for that account. The collector rejects a payload whose `transcript_path` lies under a different `<config dir>/projects/` tree, falling back to `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` in its environment when no transcript path is present. Claude Code's subprocess env scrub can remove `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, so a payload with neither signal is accepted. An account without a config directory keeps the unchecked binding. The transcript path is used only for this check and is never stored.\n**Personal Cursor subscription quota is not supported.** The team API does not authorize reading unrelated personal subscriptions. Anthropic API billing, Claude Enterprise analytics, Gemini API billing, context-window percentages, and local session token counts are not implemented as subscription quota sources.\nThe view shows provider-reported used percentage and reset time per returned limit window. Remaining percentage is labeled as a calculation from reported usage. Codex's response-level `ordinaryUsageAllowed` is preserved separately: explicit `false` means `blocked`, even when quota percentages are positive or reset times have elapsed. Missing permission remains unavailable, never inferred from quota. Per-bucket `rateLimitReachedType` and `spendControlReached` are retained even when a bucket has no windows; the five known reached-limit reasons are allowlisted, and unknown or malformed access fie\n\nBanked reset count, earliest known expiry, and per-credit details appear only when supplied. Missing observations and partial responses remain visible. Account email and opaque credit IDs are discarded at the adapter boundary. Observation time is captured when app-server returns. The view treats an observation older than five minutes as stale and updates freshness every 30 seconds independently of successful refreshes. Refresh is manual in the package; the standalone host can also refresh on an interval. The text-only fallback likewise includes source, observation time, freshness, calculation \n\nThe usage dashboard boundary is normalized per-source status and explicit read operations. Its sources are ChatGPT-signed-in Codex homes through the documented local app-server protocol, independently\n3. For usage, Codex uses one `CODEX_HOME` per sign-in with manual live reads. Claude Code and Antigravity use allowlisted status-line snapshots with explicit per-account collector bindings, and a Claude account can be bound to its own `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`; refresh does not advance snapshot observation times or query those providers. Cursor team profiles select an exact member ID and reference a server environment credential, never a browser token. The host provides account operations and keeps cr","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4f5d7241-972e-4c07-9c28-a8928ad520d5","timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:36:57.385Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"15:| Amp | Credit balance and daily Amp Free allowance from the documented `amp usage` command | The CLI's signed-in account, or another account's `AMP_API_KEY` named by an environment variable |\n19:| Claude Code (live, opt-in) | 5-hour, weekly, and per-model weekly limits (for example Fable) from Claude Code's undocumented `/api/oauth/usage` | The sign-in stored for each bound `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`; falls back to the status-line snapshot |\n55:The host stores profile labels and Codex home paths in `~/.config/subset/codex-profiles.json` with owner-only file permissions. Managed Codex homes live under `~/.local/share/subset/codex-accounts/`, each with owner-only directory permissions. An owner-controlled profile file may also be supplied directly, for example:\n74:The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.\n82:**Automatic installation.** For a Claude Code account bound to a config directory, or an Antigravity account, **Install collector** (on the card, in account settings, or as a checkbox when adding the account) writes the collector into that CLI's `settings.json`. It is an explicit operation: Subset first copies the file to `settings.json.subset-backup`, keeps every other key, and saves the replaced `statusLine` in an owner-only Subset file. The collector then runs that previous status line with the same input, so an existing display (for example Orca's) keeps rendering. **Uninstall** or removing the account restores the previous `statusLine` unless another tool has since replaced it. Status-line collectors only run in CLI sessions started after installation; a session that was already open keeps its old settings. Account settings report whether the collector is installed, missing, or replaced by another tool. Each collector run records only its time and outcome (`saved`, `no-quota`, or `wrong-directory`) in an owner-only `collector-runs` directory, so the dashboard can say whether the collector has not run yet (for example, a CLI session opened before installation), ran without quota, or ran from the wrong config directory. Settings that are not valid JSON are never rewritten. An account without a config directory cannot be installed automatically.\n84:For manual setup, configure that command as `statusLine.command` in the intended Claude Code account environment or Antigravity CLI's `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json`. For example, using the absolute host path and generated account ID:\n90:    \"command\"