last-good-on-error (invalid), Cursor redirect code (invalid), cross-process profile lock (not attempted), Cursor team counter drift (not attempted), usage-cli engines 22.12 (invalid, round 3).\n\n## Reset assessment: harness-account-identity\n\n1. **Invariant.** Two logins appear as one card (one read, the other in `alsoIn`) only when they are proven to be the same subscription account: same service and same account identity. For ChatGPT the identity is email + ChatGPT workspace ID, and both IDs must be known; for Claude, which exposes no workspace, it is the account email. Without proof, logins stay separate cards. Direct accounts follow the same identity, so two direct ChatGPT profiles under one email but different workspaces never collide either.\n2. **Old-head reproduction (consumer boundary).** `subset-usage status` (spawned CLI) with a fake `codex` whose `account/read` omits `workspaceRouting` (as older Codex app-servers do), and a Pi ChatGPT login for the same email and plan but workspace `ws_team_b`. Old head 6ae3c9d: one card, `alsoIn: ['pi']`, no Pi card (merged on plan). Same with a Pi login lacking any workspace.\n3. **Coordinated plan.** One identity function used by producers (direct readers' email/workspaceId; stored-login email/workspace) and the consumer (grouping): ChatGPT keys require a workspace; Claude keys use email. Remove plan and single-workspace heuristics; key direct owners by the same identity; harness-to-harness grouping uses the same keys, ungroupable logins stay alone. Failure boundary: missing identity data → separate cards, never a merge.\n4. **Verification (independent of helpers).** Spawned `status` JSON only: (a) unknown direct workspace + known harness workspace → 2 cards, no `alsoIn`; (b) harness with no workspace → 2 cards; (c) matching workspace IDs → 1 card with `alsoIn: ['pi']`. Run (a)/(b) against old head to show failure.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfqJ9Jun4u5s61gtwxiZp","attributionSkill":"use-skillplane",