9:42.185Z","session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"usage-dashboard-cli","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
{"parentUuid":"9140fc02-2bd0-4065-b367-9114025e0d21","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"eb4db07b-ad2e-4528-85c9-0b3a8078cd9e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XgNmj2SxdYyBxCp5nQPgPG","type":"tool_result","content":"74:The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.\nactions/checkout v4 11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262\nactions/setup-node v4 49933ea5288caeca8642d1e84afbd3f7d6820020","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d37ab9df-ebdb-453f-bbb2-947fd68d88c9","timestamp":"2026-10-08T19:09:43.661Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"74:The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.\nactions/checkout v4 11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262\nactions/setup-node v4 49933ea5288caeca8642d1e84afbd3f7d6820020","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage-cli/src/server.mjs","hunks":[{"oldStart":199,"oldLines":8,"newStart":199,"newLines":8,"lines":["     return readCodexProfile(profile);","   };"," ","-  // One account signed in to several harnesses gets one card: the direct ChatGPT or Claude","-  // account (or the first harness) is read, and the others are listed in `alsoIn`. Another","+  // One account signed in to several harnesses gets one card when its identity is proven: the","+  // direct ChatGPT or Claude account (or the first harness) is read, and the others are listed in `alsoIn`. Another","   // harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage, and never over an","   // explicit access denial.","   const results = new Map();"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage/README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":7,"newStart":29,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - **Claude Code:** for each account bound to a config directory, the host first runs `claude auth status` (Claude Code refreshes its own token there), then reads the stored OAuth access token from the macOS keychain item `Claude Code-credentials` (default directory) or `Claude Code-credentials-<first 8 hex of SHA-256 of the directory>`, falling back to `<dir>/.credentials.json`. It calls `https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage` read-only and shows `five_hour`, `seven_day`, and `limits[]` entries of kind `weekly_scoped` (per-model weekly limits such as Fable). Subset never calls the token endpoint: Anthropic rotates refresh tokens, so refreshing would sign Claude Code out. An expired token asks the user to open Claude Code. If no stored sign-in is found, the status-line snapshot is used."," - **Factory Droid:** reads `<factory home>/.factory/auth.v2.loginkeychain`, `auth.v2.keyring`, or `auth.v2.file`, decrypts it (AES-256-GCM) with the key from the keychain item `Factory CLI` or `auth.v2.key`, and calls `https://api.factory.ai/api/billing/limits` (or the EU host) with the account's organization. When off, a Droid account needs a Factory API key variable; Subset then calls `/api/cli/whoami` for the organization and the same limits endpoint."," - **Configured keys win:** a Factory Droid or Devin account set up with an API key variable always reads that key's account and reports an error when the variable is unset, rather than falling back to the default local sign-in.","-- **Harness logins** (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) are grouped by email and ChatGPT workspace, and join a direct ChatGPT account only when the plans agree, so a Personal and a Team workspace under one email stay separate. Cached reads are keyed by a hash of the stored login, so a replaced login is read again at once.","+- **Harness logins** (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) share a card with another login only when their identity is proven: for ChatGPT, the same email and the same ChatGPT workspace ID (from Codex `account/read` and the stored token), both known; for Claude, the same email. Plans are never used as proof, and a login with unknown identity keeps its own card, so Personal and Team workspaces under one email, or two Business workspaces on the same plan, stay separate. Cached reads are keyed by a hash of the stored login, so a replaced login is read again at once."," - **Codex** is unchanged: Codex app-server already reads its own sign-in through a documented interface."," "," Neither endpoint is documented by its provider, so either can change or stop working. Tokens stay in memory for one request and are never stored, logged, or sent to the view. macOS may show a keychain prompt the first time; choose **Always Allow** so auto-refresh does not wait on it. The approach follows Synara (MIT; `apps/server/src/providerUsage/providers`), Orca, and Zeron, which read the same stores. Subset's implementation is independent and read-only. Keychain lookup is macOS-only; elsewhere only credential files are read."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage-cli/src/server.mjs","/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage/README.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9140fc02-2bd0-4065-b367-9114025e0d21","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"usage-dashboard-cli","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
