{"parentUuid":"960e8608-24ab-4233-b746-3de58da6de1e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"eb4db07b-ad2e-4528-85c9-0b3a8078cd9e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QD3GZbon2n7FfnWhnmKjz7","type":"tool_result","content":"Validated 5 Subset workspaces.\n1791486593923 COMPLETED 240 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n1791486597207 COMPLETED 291 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n# pass 109\n# fail 0\n# pass 41\n# fail 0","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"de700348-ac4a-42a2-9ff5-8bc7a722f682","timestamp":"2026-10-08T19:10:09.913Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"Validated 5 Subset workspaces.\n1791486593923 COMPLETED 240 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n1791486597207 COMPLETED 291 FILES 0 ERRORS 0 WARNINGS 0 FILES_WITH_PROBLEMS\n# pass 109\n# fail 0\n# pass 41\n# fail 0","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/.github/workflows/publish-tag.yml","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":7,"newStart":29,"newLines":7,"lines":["     steps:","       # A manual run builds the requested tag, never the branch it was dispatched from.","       - name: Checkout release tag","-        uses: actions/checkout@v4","+        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4","         with:","           ref: refs/tags/${{ env.RELEASE_TAG }}","           fetch-depth: 0"]},{"oldStart":44,"oldLines":7,"newStart":44,"newLines":7,"lines":["           fi"," ","       - name: Setup Node.js","-        uses: actions/setup-node@v4","+        uses: actions/setup-node@49933ea5288caeca8642d1e84afbd3f7d6820020 # v4","         with:","           node-version: 22","           registry-url: https://registry.npmjs.org"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage/README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":71,"oldLines":7,"newStart":71,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," ### Dashboard view"," ","-The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, the stored-login email (read locally) matches the others, and they appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines.","+The shared view (`@subset/usage/view`) shows a summary strip (accounts needing attention, the soonest reset, the highest usage), then one card per account with segmented meters, reset countdowns (exact time on hover), status pills, and inline callouts. The values marked `≈` are calculated; all others are provider-reported. The pace line is a linear estimate from the average rate since the window started and is hidden during the first 5% of a window. Presentation logic lives in `@subset/usage/present` and is unit tested. Clicking a reset time or the banked-reset count opens a resets dialog with the exact reset time, window length and start, time elapsed, used and remaining values, the pace estimate, and banked resets. Card footers show only the last refresh time; the source and age are in its tooltip. `percentMode` switches every value, meter, trend, and the summary between used and remaining. An optional `email` appears under the plan when the provider's local tool reports one (Codex `account/read`, `claude auth status`); it is left out of the agent text fallback. Hosts can pass `toolbar`, `notice`, and `emptyAction` snippets, `header={false}` when they render their own app bar, an `openAccount(id)` callback, and `accountHint(account)` to explain or fix an account that is waiting for data. The standalone host adds an app bar with Usage and Settings pages, an account dialog, and per-account settings with collector setup. Settings hold the percentage display (used or remaining), auto-refresh (off, 5, 15, or 30 minutes; default 5, paused while the tab is hidden), the account list, and clearing usage history. Settings also hold the theme (system, light, dark) and whether trend charts show. Display preferences are stored in browser `localStorage` as per-viewer conveniences. Account names are optional; an unnamed account shows its email, and the default Codex and Claude Code sign-ins carry a **Default** tag. Cards show a status pill in the footer only when something needs attention. Text uses the Twenty One Native typeface from `@21n/fonts@1.0.1`, bundled by the host. Icons are from `phosphor-svelte@3.1.0` (Phosphor). Provider logos come from the CC0 Iconify `logos` set, plus the Factory, Amp, and Pi marks vendored from their own sites in `scripts/logos` (script-free SVGs, regenerated by `scripts/generate-provider-logos.mjs`). Light and dark themes follow the system setting. The first summary card is a 7-day activity heatmap built from local history: one column per day and one row per part of the day (night, morning, afternoon, evening), shaded by the usage added. Hovering or focusing a tile shows the period, points added, and the accounts that used the most. Account names can be edited or cleared from the account settings dialog; when an account has a name, its email moves into the provider and plan line, alongside the logos of other harnesses using it. When one ChatGPT or Claude account is signed in to several harnesses, the host shows one card: the direct account (or the first harness) is read, logins whose identity is proven the same (see Local sign-ins) appear as \"Also in\" logos; another harness is read only as a fallback when the main read has no usage. ChatGPT accounts are labeled ChatGPT and Claude accounts Claude; \"Codex\" and \"Claude Code\" name only the CLIs that read them. The standalone host's footer links to 21n.co and the public GitHub repository; usage.subset.dev is shown as plain text until that site is deployed (`USAGE_SITE_LIVE` in `apps/usage/src/App.svelte`). Accounts without usage windows (such as Amp) always show their balances, even with credits hidden. Harness accounts (Pi, OpenCode, omp, Hermes) carry a `service` naming the subscription they read: the provider filter and the Accounts card's donut chart group them under that service while the card keeps the harness logo. The filter row's options button holds sort order and the trend and credits toggles, which Settings also offers. Accounts that report limit groups side by side show one group per tab: Factory's standard and Core limits, and Antigravity's `<group>-<span>` buckets (Gemini and Third-party), whose span suffix also sets the window length. The Limits summary card lists exhausted windows (0% left) and running-low windows (85% or more used) as separate rows; hovering shows the affected accounts and clicking opens the full list, where each entry scrolls to its card. Accounts can be pinned from the card's options menu (shown under a Pinned filter chip and with a pin in the card footer) and sorted by default order, most recent use (latest rise in local history), or soonest reset; pins and sort order are per-browser conveniences. Trend charts mark resets, detected as sharp drops in recorded usage, with dashed lines."," "," Trend charts use Apache ECharts (`echarts@6.1.0`, SVG renderer), loaded on demand so the first page load stays small. The standalone host records local history in `usage-history.json` in its data directory (owner-only): only account IDs, window keys, observation times, and used percentages, kept for eight days and thinned when unchanged. Labels, plans, errors, and provider payloads are not stored. A card shows a trend once a window has three observations across at least 30 minutes."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/.github/workflows/publish-tag.yml","/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset/packages/usage/README.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"960e8608-24ab-4233-b746-3de58da6de1e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/subset","sessionId":"ccf55d8d-6176-4456-a8a3-555947c35bfe","version":"2.1.294","gitBranch":"usage-dashboard-cli","slug":"declarative-hopping-teapot"}
